Java
class Solution {

    public int maximalRectangle(char[][] matrix) {

        if (matrix.length == 0)
            return 0;

        int rows = matrix.length;
        int cols = matrix[0].length;

        int[] heights = new int[cols];

        int maxArea = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {

            for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {

                heights[j] =
                    matrix[i][j] == '1'
                    ? heights[j] + 1
                    : 0;
            }

            maxArea = Math.max(maxArea,
                    bruteHistogram(heights));
        }

        return maxArea;
    }

    int bruteHistogram(int[] h) {

        int max = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < h.length; i++) {

            int left = i;
            int right = i;

            while (left > 0 && h[left - 1] >= h[i])
                left--;

            while (right < h.length - 1 &&
                   h[right + 1] >= h[i])
                right++;

            max = Math.max(max,
                    h[i] * (right - left + 1));
        }

        return max;
    }
}

Complexity
Time : O(m × n²)
Space : O(n)

Approach 3: Histogram + Monotonic Stack ⭐ (Most Optimal)
Intuition

For every row:

Update histogram heights.
Solve Largest Rectangle in Histogram (84) using a monotonic stack.
Keep the maximum area.
